    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d436">

                <pb facs="0535" n="529" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg529"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/20005: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Matthews</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-03-07T00:00:00+01:00">March 7,
                            1942—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received March 7—9:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1095. By way of preliminary reply to the Department’s telegram No. 982, March 6,
                    12 midnight,<anchor n="55" xml:id="d436-f1"/> I may say that reports which have
                    reached me from a number of sources of Sir Stafford Cripps’ private discussion
                    with members of Parliament confirm that he did advocate the recognition by Great
                    Britain of Soviet frontiers as they existed on June 22, 1941.<anchor n="56"
                        xml:id="d436-f2"/> I shall endeavor to cable more fully next week concerning
                    his talk.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d436-f3"/> In the interim the Department may
                    desire to refer to my telegrams No. 1058, March 5, 11 p.m., and No. 1082, March
                        7,<anchor n="58" xml:id="d436-f4"/> both on the subject of Anglo-Soviet
                    relations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Matthews</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d436-f1" n="55" xml:id="d436-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it requested information about a speech by Sir Stafford Cripps in
                    which “he advocated the recognition by Great Britain of Soviet frontiers as they
                    existed on June 22, 1941.” (740.0011 European War 1939/20030a)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d436-f2" n="56" xml:id="d436-f2-content">
                <p>Sir Stafford’s remarks were made at a private, all-party gathering attended by
                    300 members of Parliament on February 18, 1942.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d436-f3" n="57" xml:id="d436-f3-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 1116, March 9, 7 p.m., from the Chargé in the United Kingdom,
                        <ref target="#pg530">p. 530</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d436-f4" n="58" xml:id="d436-f4-content">
                <p>Latter not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
